```jsx
function Counter() {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  useEffect(() => {
    console.log('Count changed:', count);
  }, [count]);

  return (
    <View style={styles.container}>
      <Text>Count: {count}</Text>
      <Button title="+" onPress={() => setCount(c => c + 1)} />
    </View>
  );
}
```

`useState` gives this component its own piece of local, persistent state — surviving re-renders the same way `State` does in Flutter or `@State` does in SwiftUI. `useEffect` with a `[count]` dependency array runs its callback only when `count` actually changes, not on every render — getting that dependency array wrong (omitting a value it actually reads, or leaving it empty when it shouldn't be) is one of React's most common correctness bugs, on web or native.

React Native's layout model is Flexbox — the same model as CSS Flexbox, adapted with one key default flip: `flexDirection` defaults to `column` (not `row`, as on the web), matching how most mobile screens stack content vertically by default.

```jsx
const styles = StyleSheet.create({
  row: { flexDirection: 'row', justifyContent: 'space-between', padding: 12 },
});
```

There's no CSS cascade, no selectors, and no separate stylesheet language — `StyleSheet.create` is just a plain JavaScript object, and styles apply only to the exact component they're passed to, explicitly, with no inheritance from ancestors the way CSS classes cascade. Anyone coming from web CSS has to unlearn cascading and relearn that every style is local unless explicitly passed down as a prop.

```jsx
// Wrong: a brand-new function and object are created every render
<ExpensiveList
  onItemPress={(id) => handlePress(id)}
  style={{ marginTop: 8 }}
/>
```

Even if `ExpensiveList` is wrapped in `React.memo` to skip re-rendering when its props haven't changed, a new arrow function and a new object literal are created on **every** parent render — React's shallow prop comparison sees a 'different' function and a 'different' object every time (different reference, even if functionally identical), so memoization never actually skips anything.

The fix is stabilizing references: `useCallback` for the function, a `StyleSheet.create` object or a `useMemo`'d style object defined outside the render (or memoized) instead of an inline literal. This is one of the most common reasons a `React.memo`-wrapped component 'doesn't seem to help' in practice — the optimization is correctly implemented but silently defeated by unstable prop references passed into it.
